23:15:20 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: It’s a common myth that *everyone* in the 1990s thought the Internet was a passing fad. In reality, there was a wide spectrum of opinion—from visionary enthusiasm to deep skepticism. However, a highly vocal segment of pundits, business leaders, and even technologists did dismiss it as overhyped and destined to fail massively. Their reasoning wasn’t just stubbornness; it was grounded in the technological, economic, and cultural context of the time. Here are the main reasons why many were convinced the Internet wouldn’t last. ### 1. The technology was painfully primitive In the early-to-mid 1990s, the consumer Internet was slow, ugly, and frustrating. Dial-up modems screeched, pages loaded line by line over many seconds or even minutes, and graphics were tiny and low-resolution. Streaming video was a fantasy. To most people accustomed to polished media like television, glossy magazines, and CD-ROMs, the Internet seemed like a downgrade. Skeptics argued: - “Why would anyone wait minutes to read text on a screen when you can pick up a newspaper?” - “It’s slower and uglier than using a fax machine or a phone.” They couldn’t foresee broadband, compression, and mobile connectivity making multimedia seamless. ### 2. The early killer app wasn’t obvious Before the Web exploded (around 1993–94), the Internet’s primary uses were email, Usenet newsgroups, file transfer (FTP), and Gopher. To a business executive or a typical consumer, these seemed like niche tools for academics and nerds. Email was useful, but many companies already had internal systems. The idea of a global “information superhighway” sounded abstract. Without Facebook, Google, YouTube, or Amazon, it was hard to imagine what you’d actually *do* online that you couldn’t do more easily offline. ### 3. The dot-com boom and inevitable bust By the late 1990s, hype had swung the other way—companies with no revenue and a “.com” in their name received astronomical valuations. This speculative frenzy made thoughtful observers (and some burned by the 1987 stock crash) deeply suspicious. When the bubble burst in 2000, many took it as proof that the Internet was a speculative mania akin to tulip bulbs, not a sustainable economic shift. They failed to see that the underlying infrastructure and user habits were still growing, and that the crash killed overvalued companies, not the network itself. ### 4. Influential, quotable naysayers A few high-profile skeptics gave the “fad” idea its staying power: - **Paul Krugman** (1998): “By 2005 or so, it will become clear that the Internet’s impact on the economy has been no greater than the fax machine’s.” - **Clifford Stoll** (Newsweek, 1995): In a now-famous essay titled *Why the Web Won’t Be Nirvana*, he dismissed online shopping (“no way to try on a sweater”), digital news (“no scent of ink”), e-books (“unpleasant to read on a screen”), and virtual communities (“isolating, not connecting”). He was a respected astronomer and network security expert, so his arguments carried weight. - **Ethernet inventor Bob Metcalfe** (1995) predicted the Internet would “catastrophically collapse” in 1996 due to growth outstripping capacity. He literally ate his words (blending a printed column into a drink) when it didn’t. ### 5. Analogy to past failed media revolutions History buffs pointed to technologies that were hyped as world-changing but flopped or remained niche: - **CB radio** in the 1970s: huge fad, then collapsed. - **Videotex** (like Minitel in France, or Prestel in the UK): walled gardens that let you shop and bank via terminal. They seemed like the “internet of the future” but failed in most countries—why would a global, open network succeed where corporate-backed ones didn’t? - **VR in the 90s**: enormous hype, but technology didn’t deliver; it became a joke. The Internet seemed similarly overpromised. ### 6. Concerns about security, privacy, and trust For the average person, putting credit card details into a computer was terrifying. Horror stories about hackers, viruses, and pedophiles dominated early coverage. Many believed that no sane person would ever conduct financial transactions or share personal information online. The idea that we’d one day do banking, taxes, and dating on the Internet seemed absurd when identity theft and a lack of encryption were real fears. ### 7. The “walled garden” competition In the mid-90s, proprietary services like America Online (AOL), CompuServe, and Prodigy offered a curated, safer, and easier experience. Many assumed these controlled ecosystems would win, while the open, chaotic, and porn-riddled Internet would remain a sideshow for hobbyists. They didn’t anticipate that the open Web’s flexibility would ultimately trump walled gardens. ### 8. It felt like a solitary, sedentary hobby Critics like Stoll argued the Internet would create a generation of isolated zombies staring at screens, replacing genuine human interaction. They saw it as a poor substitute for real-world communities—an anti-social fad that would burn out once people realized how empty it felt. (Social media later proved it could be extremely engaging, though the debate about its social effects remains.) ### So, did *everyone* think it was a fad? Absolutely not. In the 1990s, millions of users were pouring onto the Web, scientists and academics had relied on it for decades, and venture capitalists were pouring billions into its infrastructure. Visionaries like Tim Berners-Lee, Marc Andreessen, and even Bill Gates (after his initial hesitation) saw it as a revolution. The “fad” narrative is largely a retrospective exaggeration, fed by a handful of memorable, wrong predictions. In short, the skepticism was driven by a mix of limited early technology, a lack of imagination about future applications, the trauma of the dot-com crash, and a very human tendency to judge a new medium by the standards of the old ones it would eventually displace.
